body:
**Shape.** `var b := a; using var p := b;` makes `p` a borrow-pin of `b`'s source `a` (§28.10 *initialized from a borrow*); a transfer of `a` while `p` is in scope must be rejected by the consume-site freeze. r35 accepts it.

```bezel
module Probe;

public sealed class Q
  private _id: int;
  public constructor(id: int)
    _id := id;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
end Q;

public sealed class R
  private _id: int;
  public owned f: Q;
  public constructor(id: int, owned q: Q)
    _id := id;
    f := q;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
  public static method Take(owned r: R)
    using var k := move r;
  end Take;
  public static method Body()
    var a := new R(2, new Q(1));
    var b := a;                       // b borrows a
    using var p := b;                 // borrow-pin: §28.10 says the source stays frozen to p's scope end
    R.Take(move a);                   // a transfer of the pinned source: must be rejected (K5.1); accepted
    p.Use();                          // reads through the pin after the transfer
  end Body;
end R;

public class Program
  public static method Main(): int
    R.Body();
    Console.WriteLine("END");
    return 0;
  end Main;
end Program;
```

**Measured on r35.** Compiles with zero diagnostics in both modes. `--checked-disposal`: `D:2 D:1` then `Unhandled exception: System.DisposedAccessException`, exit 70 (the read through the pin after `Take` disposed the object). `--unchecked-disposal`: `D:2 D:1 U:0 END` — a read of freed memory. With a sink that keeps the object alive (a domain transfer) the read is a cross-domain data race with no backstop.

Without the pin, `var b := a; Take(move a); b.Use();` is correctly rejected (`cannot consume \`a\` here: the borrow held by \`b\` is still live`). So the direct borrow is traced and the pin over it is not — the pin's required liveness (to its scope end) is not added to the freeze's liveness.

**Spec (v0.345), §28.10 *`using var` initialized from a borrow*.** *"The `using var` still **pins** the borrow's source for the duration of the scope … the borrow stays **live** until the using-var's scope ends, exactly as if it were used at every point up to that end, and the consume-site freeze then governs every consume of the source in that range — the frozen kills (a move, a transfer, a reassignment of the binding) are compile errors"*. K5.5: liveness traces *"borrows … through `using var` pins"*. Expected: the K5.1 freeze error at `Take(move a)`.

**Note.** On r35 an unmarked `using var p := a;` over the *owned* `a` is also still admitted as a borrow-pin (#5582, open spec-tracking); that form has the same hole (`using var p := a; Take(move a); p.Use();`).

**Measured on r35** (`bezelc 0.0.0-f6863e62`, the 2026-10-27 release build; main may have moved since). Found by the model-vs-compiler differential (`scratch/bzdiff`, proposal 0047 §5.3 / soundness README §3.2): the executable ownership model (bzcore L1, spec v0.345) and `bezelc` disagree on this program; the model's verdict is the spec's sentence quoted above.
